<exercise label="counting-simple-committee-seating">
    <title>Committee Seating Arrangements</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Eight committee members are meeting in a room that has twelve chairs.
        </p>
    </statement>

    <!-- Part 1: Combinations (Choosing the chairs) -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In how many distinct ways can the group choose which <m>8</m> chairs will be occupied, if we do not care yet who sits in which chair?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><m>\binom{12}{8} = 495</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! Since we are only choosing a subset of chairs to be occupied and the order does not matter, we use the combination formula <m>C(12,8)</m>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><m>P(12,8) = 19,958,400</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This counts the number of ways to assign specific people to specific chairs, which factors in order.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><m>12 \times 8 = 96</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This applies the multiplication rule incorrectly instead of choosing a subset.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><m>8! = 40,320</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This is the number of ways to arrange the 8 members linearly, but it doesn't account for choosing from the 12 chairs.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Does the order in which the empty chairs are left over matter, or are we just selecting a subset?
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: Permutations (Assigning people to chairs) -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In how many distinct ways can the <m>8</m> individual committee members actually sit down in the <m>12</m> available chairs?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><m>P(12,8) = 19,958,400</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Correct! Since each committee member is distinct, the order in which they occupy the chairs matters. We use permutations, or calculate <m>495 \times 8!</m>.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><m>\binom{12}{8} = 495</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This only selects which chairs are used, treating all committee members as if they were identical.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><m>12^8 = 429,981,696</m> ways</statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>
                        Incorrect. This would allow multiple committee members to sit in the same chair, which is not physically possible.
                    </p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Think about the options available for the first person, then the second person, and so on, until all 8 are seated.
            </p>
        </hint>
    </task>
</exercise>


