<exercise label="counting-simple-exam-probability">
    <title>Exam Problem Probability</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A student prepares for an exam by studying a list of ten problems. She can solve six of them. For the exam, the instructor selects five problems at random from the ten on the list given to the students.
        </p>
        <p>
            What is the probability that the student can solve all five problems on the exam?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
                <m>\frac{1}{42}</m> (or approximately <m>0.0238</m>)
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Correct! The total number of ways to choose 5 problems out of 10 is <m>\binom{10}{5} = 252</m>. The number of favorable ways to choose 5 problems from the 6 she knows is <m>\binom{6}{5} = 6</m>. Therefore, the probability is <m>\frac{6}{252} = \frac{1}{42}</m>.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> (or <m>0.5000</m>)
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This assumes a simple linear probability ratio (<m>5/10</m>) instead of evaluating the combinations of choosing groups of questions without replacement.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>\frac{5}{6}</m> (or approximately <m>0.8333</m>)
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. This is the ratio of exam problems to known problems, but it doesn't represent the likelihood of drawing all five from her known pool.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
            <statement>
                <m>\frac{1}{252}</m> (or approximately <m>0.0040</m>)
            </statement>
            <feedback>
                <p>
                    Incorrect. You correctly calculated the total sample space size (<m>\binom{10}{5} = 252</m>), but there is more than just <m>1</m> way to choose <m>5</m> questions out of the <m>6</m> she prepared.
                </p>
            </feedback>
        </choice>
    </choices>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Use the hypergeometric approach or combination formulas. Find the total number of ways to choose <m>5</m> questions from <m>10</m> (the denominator) and the number of ways to choose <m>5</m> questions entirely out of the <m>6</m> she knows how to solve (the numerator).
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            To find the probability, we divide the number of successful combinations by the total possible combinations:
        </p>
        <p>
            1. Total ways the instructor can choose 5 problems out of 10: 
            <me>\binom{10}{5} = \frac{10 \times 9 \times 8 \times 7 \times 6}{5 \times 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1} = 252</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            2. Favorable ways the instructor can select 5 problems that the student knows (from her pool of 6):
            <me>\binom{6}{5} = 6</me>
        </p>
        <p>
            3. Calculating the probability:
            <me>P(\text{all 5 solved}) = \frac{\binom{6}{5}}{\binom{10}{5}} = \frac{6}{252} = \frac{1}{42} \approx 0.0238</me>
        </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

